<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego de Almagro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1475–<a href="/years/1538">1538</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego de Almagro was a Spanish conquistador and partner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a> south of Pizarro's lands, and he led the first Spanish expedition into Chile in 1535–1537. His claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> led to war with the Pizarros, who defeated him at Las Salinas in 1538 and had him executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro.jpg" alt="Diego de Almagro" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domingo Mesa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_de_Almagro.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 1537 Manco repulsed a Spanish attack at Ollantaytambo by flooding the plain below its terraces, but the return of <b>Diego de Almagro</b>’s army from Chile in April made the Sacred Valley untenable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…divided the conquered lands into the governorates of New Castile, granted to Pizarro, and New Toledo, granted to <b>Diego de Almagro</b>, but rivalry between the two conquerors led to civil war, the execution of Almagro in 1538 and the assassination of Pizarro in 1541 in Lima, the city…</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Diego de Almagro</b>’s expedition was turned back after the Battle of Reynogüelén in 1536, and Pedro de Valdivia founded Santiago in 1541, where the northern Mapuche leader Michimalonco, who had recently defeated the Inca governor, attacked and destroyed the town on 11 September 1541.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishop was sailing to Peru to settle a dispute between Francisco Pizarro and <b>Diego de Almagro</b> when the winds died and strong currents carried his ship out to the Galápagos Islands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The boundary with <b>Diego de Almagro's</b> Governorate of New Toledo, especially the possession of Cusco, led to civil war, and Almagro was executed in 1538.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-castile">Governorate of New Castile</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1534 Charles V granted <b>Diego de Almagro</b>, Francisco Pizarro's partner in the conquest of Peru, a governorate of 200 leagues south of the Governorate of New Castile.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Almagro, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-3.71&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Almagro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4458 769 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diego de Almagro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4908 1039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4908 1039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-3.71&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
